{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-124194",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n4\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions we can write the series as\n\n$$\n4\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=8,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{8} & =4\\cdot [(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{9})] \\\\ {s}_{9} & =4\\cdot [(\\frac{1}{8}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\frac{1}{10})] \\\\ {s}_{10} & =4\\cdot [(\\frac{1}{8}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{10}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{10}}-\\frac{1}{11})] \\\\ {s}_{11} & =4\\cdot [(\\frac{1}{8}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{9}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{10}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{10}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{11}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{11}}-\\frac{1}{12})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=4\\cdot [\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=8}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }4\\cdot [\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\frac{1}{8}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
